SHIORI 1

// ---- Chapter Three: Lantern Night. The comic set-piece, then the glass cracks. --

# festival "Lantern Night"
@chapter "Chapter Three" subtitle="Operation Paper Lantern"
@pov nagi
@scene square with=fade time=1.5
@music paper_lantern fade=1
@weather fireflies layer=back intensity=0.8
Lantern Night. Every eave in the square is strung with lanterns of coloured glass, so the town looks like the inside of a jewellery box. Grilled squid and burnt sugar on the air.
Step one of Operation Paper Lantern comes running across the square with flour on her face.
@show hinata scared at=center
hinata (scared): Okay. Okay. Don't be angry.
@show nemu sleepy at=right
nemu (serious): I'm always angry. It just takes too much effort to show it. What happened?
hinata (scared): I took the council the bread, like you said. Forty loaves.
nemu (surprised): Forty. I said enough to keep them busy.
hinata (grin): That's what forty loaves {i}is!{/i} Do you know how much the council eats?
nemu (serious): Where are the loaves now, Hinata?
hinata (blush): ...They said it was too generous to keep to themselves. They're bringing it here. To hand out. As a civic gesture. Your mother was very moved.
nemu (sleepy): So the mayor is coming. Here. To the exact place where we are committing the crime.
nemu (sleepy): Fine. Step three still holds. Where's our countess?
@hide hinata
@hide nemu
@cg cg_countess with=dissolve time=1
She has made an entrance. Of course she has.
The Keeper of Glass Hill is wearing Mayor Kagami's spare festival kimono, which is purple, with ducks on it. A white fox mask over her glass eyes. A straw hat the size of a cartwheel.
And because nobody in Shiomi owns opera gloves, her glass hands are hidden in a pair of Hinata's oven mitts, with ribbons tied round the cuffs.
suzu: Is it hideous? Describe it. I want to know exactly how hideous.
nagi: It's purple. It has ducks.
suzu: Magnificent. Oh ho ho! Tonight I am the Countess Fluorite, of the Capital. A widow. Tragically.
nagi: What happened to the count?
suzu: Eaten by a lion.
nemu: There are no lions in the capital.
suzu: There are now. That's how tragic it was.
@cg none with=dissolve time=0.8
@show suzu proud at=center
@show nemu smirk at=right
@show hinata grin at=left
She will not be led. A countess, she explains, leads. She walks into the crowd by sound alone, and it parts for the hat.
@camera zoom=1.2 x=0.15 time=1
At the goldfish stall she kneels, tilts her head toward the tank, and listens. She scoops nine goldfish in a row. Her paper scoop never tears. The stall keeper starts to cry.
suzu (proud): The fish fear me!
@camera reset time=1
hinata (grin): She's {i}incredible.{/i}
nemu (sleepy): She's conspicuous. They look alike from a distance.
@if knows_wish
  Under the noise, she tugs my sleeve with a mitt.
  suzu (soft): Is the sea near?
  nagi: Two streets.
  suzu (sad): ...Not tonight. One hour. Another time.
-> bell_contest

# bell_contest "The Glass Bell Contest"
@move suzu at=midleft time=0.8
In the middle of the square, forty wind chimes hang on a bamboo frame for the Glass Bell Contest. Master Sekiguchi, who has judged it for thirty years and won it for twenty, walks the row with his hands behind his back.
The Countess's head comes up like a dog hearing its name.
suzu (proud): That one's a quarter-tone flat and ashamed of itself.
People turn. She doesn't notice, or she does and loves it.
suzu (proud): That one's lovely, but it's lying. That one was tuned by a man who has never been loved. That one— oh, that one's simply a spoon.
@move nemu at=farright time=0.6
nemu (panic): The plan. The plan was a low profile.
"Master Sekiguchi": Madam. You seem to have opinions.
suzu (proud): I am the Countess Fluorite, of the Capital. I have nothing else.
"Master Sekiguchi": Then perhaps the Countess would judge blindfolded, and tell us whose chime is whose.
suzu (proud): A blindfold. Oh ho ho. How quaint.
They tie the cloth. The crowd presses in. Somebody rings the first chime.
suzu (proud): Master Sekiguchi's own. It rings just like something he gave m—
She stops for a hair of a second.
suzu (proud): —like a man who is very sure of himself.
She names the maker of every chime on the frame, and by the thirtieth the crowd is chanting {i}Coun-tess, Coun-tess.{/i}
Master Sekiguchi, defeated, asks her to choose the winner. She walks the row once, past the guild's best work, and stops at the very end: a lopsided little chime with a crooked clapper, entered by {i}Kenji, age 7.{/i}
suzu (soft): That one. It's crooked, and it's honest. Somebody made it for somebody.
The square goes mad. A small boy is lifted onto shoulders. Hinata is crying with laughter.
nemu (sleepy): We have achieved the opposite of a low profile. This is a profile visible from the moon.
Then the crowd parts again, the polite way it parts for officials. Two council men come through with baskets of bread. Behind them, smiling, comes Mayor Kagami.
nemu (panic): Ah. So this is the part of the plan where I emigrate.
-> the_crack

# the_crack
kagami: What a wonderful thing! Countess, on behalf of Shiomi, may I?
The mayor holds out her hand.
And the Countess Fluorite, who has never in her life been allowed to shake anybody's hand for its own sake, holds out hers.
The mayor shakes it warmly. The oven mitt slides off.
@music stop fade=0.2
@camera zoom=1.4 x=-0.05 time=0.4
The Keeper's hand is in Mayor Kagami's: glass, clear as water, with the lantern light caught inside it like a fish.
It's very quiet. I can hear the river, and a baby, and the fizz of a candle in the nearest lantern.
kagami: ...Keeper.
suzu (surprised): Do you hear that?
@camera reset time=0.3
@shake strength=1.5 time=1
@flash color=#ffffff time=0.6
Up on Glass Hill, the Glasshouse roof flashes once, as if someone has lit a lamp inside it. Then the sound reaches us: a long splintering chime, like ten thousand cups set down at once.
@music small_hours fade=0.5
@tint #3a1a4a alpha=0.35 time=0.5
@weather embers intensity=0.6
All over the square, people stop.
At the goldfish stall Mr. Imai looks down at the top of his son's head. Then, carefully, as if carrying something that might go off, he hands the baby to a stranger and walks away fast toward the dark harbor. Nobody stops him.
By the shaved-ice stand an old woman starts to laugh, high and delighted, on and on. I never find out who she is.
A schoolboy slaps his little sister, hard, and then stares at his own hand.
@show hinata scared at=left
@shake strength=0.6 time=0.4
Hinata has dropped a tray of melon bread. She's staring at Taichi in the sling on her back as if he were a snake.
hinata (scared): Take him. Mame. Take him off me. Take him off, take him, {shake}take him—{/shake}
@show suzu sad at=right
Two council men already have the Keeper by the arms. Her mask has slipped. She turns her glass eyes toward the hill and says, quite calmly:
suzu (sad): It's the newest shelf. This year's. Oh, Hinata.
kagami: Take her back up the hill. Now.
The mayor doesn't shout. That's the worst part. She says it in the voice you'd use to send a dish back to the kitchen.

* Run to Hinata.
  @set ran_to = "hinata"
  @set hinata_bond += 1
  @hide suzu
  @move hinata at=center time=0.4
  I get to Hinata before her knees go. I lift Taichi out of the sling and put him in Mame's arms, and Mame takes him without a word.
  Hinata is bigger than me. I carry her home anyway, mostly by refusing to put her down. I sit with her on the flour sacks until she falls asleep, near midnight, with her apron strings knotted round her wrists.
  Then I go up the hill.
* Run after the Keeper.
  @set ran_to = "suzu"
  @set suzu_bond += 1
  @hide hinata
  I run after the Keeper. The council men take the steps two at a time with her between them, her bare feet not touching. At the top they bar her into the kiln-house.
  kagami: Go home, Nagi. You of all people.
  I don't know what she means. I sit down on the step anyway. Below me, the town is making a sound I have never heard it make.
-> sleeve
